From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 01:33:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: bound the command allocator storage kept between
 recordings

A Metal 4 command allocator keeps its storage at its high-water mark when
it is reset, and releasing it is the only way to return that storage. The
device cached up to 24 reset allocators with no limit on their size, and
every command buffer took an allocator for each of its three lanes when it
was created or begun and kept them across recordings, whether or not it
recorded anything. A GL driver begins spare command buffers it seldom
uses, so 150 s into a Plasma desktop session on a Mac, 255 allocators
held 3.6 GiB of command storage, the largest 1.1 GiB, while no recording
needed it.

A command buffer now takes an allocator for a lane only when that lane
opens a Metal command buffer. Every submission, not only a one-time one,
takes the allocators it recorded into along with its Metal command buffers
and hands them to the device cache when Metal reports it complete, and a
reset hands the cache only allocators that were never submitted. The cache
recycles only allocators of at most 16 MiB, since a recording that takes a
grown one keeps all of that storage however little it records. It keeps at
most 64 MiB in total and releases every other allocator.

A command buffer in the initial state therefore holds no allocator, so the
driver's vkResetCommandBuffer, which reset initial buffers too so that a
release reached their allocators, goes, and the common entrypoint applies.

Handing allocators to a shared cache makes one case unsafe that was merely
wrong before. Vulkan lets a SIMULTANEOUS_USE command buffer be resubmitted
while its last submission is still running, and Metal cannot run a
recording twice, so the driver records the buffer again. That re-record
frees the uploads, large BOs and descriptor buffers the running submission
reads, and it used to reset the lane's allocator under it; with the cache,
another command buffer could also take that allocator and record into it.
The command buffer now keeps a reference to its last submission, and a
re-record waits for Metal to report that submission before it frees
anything. A queue that stops answering loses the device after the bound
the timeline and query waits give it, because a submit on the caller's
thread would otherwise wait with nothing else to notice. The new
kosmickrisp_command_resubmit test holds a first submission on the GPU
behind a timeline wait and requires the resubmission to wait for it.

With the previous commit, the same session peaks at one 257 MiB allocator
during its first frame and then keeps 39 MiB in 9 allocators, none larger
than 6 MiB. Without the 16 MiB recycling limit it kept 61 MiB, 41 MiB of
it in two allocators larger than 16 MiB.
